# Project overview
This project is a clone of **Synopsys PrimeTime**, the gate-level **Static Timing Analysis (STA)** tool.
It is not a streaming, TV, or consumer application.
## Goal
Reproduce the core PrimeTime workflow: read a design and its timing data, build a timing graph,
propagate arrival and required times, and report timing (slack, critical paths, violations)
through a PrimeTime-like Tcl command interface.
## Simplified-first principle
Every feature starts as a simplified, well-understood model and is refined in later stages.
Do not jump to the elaborate production-grade algorithm before the simplified version exists and is validated.
| Area | Initial model | Later refinements (not yet in scope) |
|------|---------------|--------------------------------------|
| Net (interconnect) delay | Elmore delay on RC trees | AWE, moment matching, effective capacitance (Ceff), CCS-style driver models |
| Cell delay and slew | NLDM table lookup with interpolation | CCS / ECSM current-source models |
Apply the same principle to any feature not listed: pick the simplest correct model first,
and record the intended refinement path in the plan.
## Textual interfaces in scope
The tool's inputs and outputs are standard EDA text formats. Their specifications will be gathered
during planning and are the reference for any parser or writer:
- Liberty (`.lib`): cell timing and power libraries
- Structural Verilog: gate-level netlists
- SDC: timing constraints (clocks, I/O delays, exceptions)
- SPEF: parasitics (RC networks used for net delay)
- SDF: delay annotation (read and/or write)
- Tcl: the PrimeTime-style command shell and scripts
## Intellectual property
Base behavior on public specifications and publicly documented command semantics.
Do not copy proprietary Synopsys source code, documentation text, or trademarked material verbatim.